# Device nodes baked into the image, in mksquashfs -pf syntax.
#
# They have to be here rather than made at boot. mdev populates /dev from
# sysfs, but /dev is a tmpfs mounted BY rcS -- which needs /dev/null and a
# console before mdev has run, and MTD character devices are not exposed
# through sysfs in a way mdev acts on anyway. The vendor's image ships static
# nodes for the same reason.
#
# THESE ARE ALSO THE SOURCE FOR /etc/scripts/make-devices.sh, which the build
# generates from this file and rcS runs after mounting the tmpfs on /dev. Both
# are needed: the squashfs copy is what /dev holds before rcS mounts anything,
# and the generated script is what puts them back afterwards. Until 2026-09-14
# only the squashfs copy existed and the tmpfs mount hid every one of them.
#
# mksquashfs applies these without root, so the build needs no privileges.
#
#   <path> <type> <mode> <uid> <gid> <major> <minor>
#
# /dev itself is a real directory in the staging tree, not declared here: when
# both exist mksquashfs keeps the real one and warns about the pseudo entry.

# The four every program assumes exist.
/dev/console c 600 0 0 5 1
/dev/null    c 666 0 0 1 3
/dev/zero    c 666 0 0 1 5
/dev/tty     c 666 0 0 5 0

# The kernel log ring, write only (drivers/char/mem.c:906,946 registers minor
# 11 unconditionally and gives it a .write and no .read). This image runs NO
# syslogd -- busybox is built without the applet -- so /dev/log has no reader
# and everything that calls syslog(3) writes into nothing. That is how a
# dropbear that failed to daemonise left no trace anywhere. /dev/kmsg is the
# one place a boot-time failure can be put where dmesg still has it later.
/dev/kmsg c 600 0 0 1 11

# Entropy, and there is only ONE node here on purpose.
#
# /dev/urandom has to be static: it has no sysfs entry, so nothing populating
# /dev from /sys will ever create it. Without it dropbearkey exits with
# "Failure reading random device /dev/urandom" and the stick comes up with no
# host key and no ssh. Reproduced by test/rootfs_chroot_test.sh.
#
# /dev/random IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. It is the blocking pool, and on this
# machine a one-byte read never returns -- measured on the booted image, where
# an unbounded read killed the audit probe. A read of it inside rcS would hang
# sysinit, and init starts no respawn entry until sysinit completes: no
# console, no telnet, no ssh, on a box that looks alive until the watchdog
# resets it. Nothing here can read it -- dropbear and dropbearkey use
# DROPBEAR_URANDOM_DEV with no fallback, and the only reference to the string
# in the whole rootfs is busybox miscutils/seedrng.c, an applet we do not
# build. So the node was a trap and nothing else: absent, a caller gets ENOENT
# and can say so; present, it hangs. The stock vendor image ships it and gets
# away with it for the same reason we did.
/dev/urandom c 666 0 0 1 9

# The Unix98 pty multiplexer. Without this node busybox telnetd cannot get a
# pty at all: it is built CONFIG_FEATURE_DEVPTS=y, so its getpty() opens
# /dev/ptmx and has no BSD fallback compiled in. rcS mounts devpts on /dev/pts
# for the slave side; this is the master.
/dev/ptmx c 666 0 0 5 2

# The serial console. ttyS0 is the one inittab respawns a login on.
/dev/ttyS0 c 600 0 0 4 64
/dev/ttyS1 c 600 0 0 4 65

# Flash. Major 90 is MTD char (minor 2n for mtdN, 2n+1 for the read-only
# alias); major 31 is mtdblock, and jffs2 needs the block node. FOURTEEN
# partitions on this board, 0..13 -- read from /proc/mtd on both sticks:
# boot, env, env2, config, k0, r0, k1, r1, four 4K placeholders, then linux
# and rootfs, which are aliases onto whichever slot is running. Nothing
# resolves a partition by number: mount-config.sh and fwu.sh both look the
# name up in /proc/mtd.
/dev/mtd0  c 600 0 0 90 0
/dev/mtd1  c 600 0 0 90 2
/dev/mtd2  c 600 0 0 90 4
/dev/mtd3  c 600 0 0 90 6
/dev/mtd4  c 600 0 0 90 8
/dev/mtd5  c 600 0 0 90 10
/dev/mtd6  c 600 0 0 90 12
/dev/mtd7  c 600 0 0 90 14
/dev/mtd8  c 600 0 0 90 16
/dev/mtd9  c 600 0 0 90 18
/dev/mtd10 c 600 0 0 90 20
/dev/mtd11 c 600 0 0 90 22
/dev/mtd12 c 600 0 0 90 24
/dev/mtd13 c 600 0 0 90 26
# The READ-ONLY aliases, on the odd minors. mtdchar.c reads the partition as
# `minor >> 1` and refuses FMODE_WRITE when `minor & 1`, so this is enforced by
# the kernel rather than by the mode bits -- opening mtd1ro for writing is
# -EACCES whoever you are. The stock vendor image ships its own eight. This is
# what docs/TRIAL-BOOT.md wants for the backup step: a mistyped redirection
# onto mtd1ro cannot destroy the environment.
/dev/mtd0ro  c 400 0 0 90 1
/dev/mtd1ro  c 400 0 0 90 3
/dev/mtd2ro  c 400 0 0 90 5
/dev/mtd3ro  c 400 0 0 90 7
/dev/mtd4ro  c 400 0 0 90 9
/dev/mtd5ro  c 400 0 0 90 11
/dev/mtd6ro  c 400 0 0 90 13
/dev/mtd7ro  c 400 0 0 90 15
/dev/mtd8ro  c 400 0 0 90 17
/dev/mtd9ro  c 400 0 0 90 19
/dev/mtd10ro c 400 0 0 90 21
/dev/mtd11ro c 400 0 0 90 23
/dev/mtd12ro c 400 0 0 90 25
/dev/mtd13ro c 400 0 0 90 27

/dev/mtdblock0  b 600 0 0 31 0
/dev/mtdblock1  b 600 0 0 31 1
/dev/mtdblock2  b 600 0 0 31 2
/dev/mtdblock3  b 600 0 0 31 3
/dev/mtdblock4  b 600 0 0 31 4
/dev/mtdblock5  b 600 0 0 31 5
/dev/mtdblock6  b 600 0 0 31 6
/dev/mtdblock7  b 600 0 0 31 7
/dev/mtdblock8  b 600 0 0 31 8
/dev/mtdblock9  b 600 0 0 31 9
/dev/mtdblock10 b 600 0 0 31 10
/dev/mtdblock11 b 600 0 0 31 11
/dev/mtdblock12 b 600 0 0 31 12
/dev/mtdblock13 b 600 0 0 31 13
